      "For nearly six years, Carlos Mendoza believed that exhaustion was simply the price of ambition. As marketing director of a rapidly growing furniture company in Madrid, he regularly worked seventy hours a week, answered messages at midnight, and cancelled holidays at the last minute with a casual remark that the business needed him. Colleagues admired him, and his managers described him as indispensable. Looking back, he now says that being called indispensable was the most dangerous compliment he ever received.",
      "The first warning signs were so ordinary that he ignored them. He began to wake at four in the morning with his heart racing, and he lost interest in the football matches that he had watched with his brother since childhood. He assumed that he needed a rest and that a long weekend would solve everything. It did not. By the spring of last year, he was making mistakes that would have been unthinkable earlier; he sent a campaign to the wrong list of customers, and in a meeting he forgot the name of a client he had worked with for four years. He laughed it off, but his assistant, Lucia, noticed that he had stopped laughing at anything else.",
      "The collapse came on a Monday in October. Standing in front of the team to present the annual budget, Carlos found that he could not read his own slides. The words seemed to move, and he felt unable to breathe. He apologised, left the room, and sat in his car for nearly two hours without being able to decide where to drive. His wife, Elena, found him there that evening. The doctor who examined him the following day did not use dramatic words; he simply said that Carlos had burnout and that his body had stopped cooperating.",
      "Recovery was slower than he expected. He took three months of sick leave, and during the first month he could barely read a newspaper article. The doctor insisted that rest alone would not be enough, so Carlos began meeting a therapist every second week, and he discovered that his exhaustion was linked to a belief that his value depended entirely on his output. Many people, the therapist told him, confuse being busy with being important. This was not a comfortable realisation, but it was a useful one.",
      "When he returned to work, Carlos negotiated changes that surprised his employer. He now works four and a half days a week, does not check email after seven in the evening, and has delegated half of his previous responsibilities to a deputy. The company, to everyone's astonishment, has not suffered; the campaign results this year are better than before. Carlos has become cautious about advising others, because he knows that not everyone has a manager who will listen. Nevertheless, he repeats one point whenever anyone asks: the person who works until midnight every day is not the hero of the company, but its most obvious risk."
